# Local development (docker compose). Production values come from Doppler (doc 09 §5) —
# never commit real secrets.
#
# TWO CONNECTIONS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS LOAD-BEARING.
#
# DATABASE_URL is the APPLICATION connection and must use `app_user`: a role that is
# neither superuser nor table owner, so row-level security actually applies. Pointing
# this at the `autobureau` superuser makes every RLS policy silently inert — the app
# would appear to work perfectly while tenant isolation did nothing. That is precisely
# the failure the architecture review caught (blocker F-01). Do not reintroduce it
# through a convenient .env.
#
# DATABASE_ADMIN_URL is for migrations and test fixtures only. It bypasses RLS by
# design. Never use it to serve a request.

# Host is 127.0.0.1, never `localhost`. On macOS `localhost` resolves to ::1 first, and a
# host postgres bound to the specific loopback addresses beats docker's wildcard bind on
# the same port — which silently routes the whole test suite at the wrong server.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://app_user:app_local_only@127.0.0.1:55432/autobureau"
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL="postgresql://autobureau:local_dev_only@127.0.0.1:55432/autobureau"
REDIS_URL="redis://127.0.0.1:63790"

# ─────────────────────────── authenticated boundary (ADR-009) ───────────────────────────
#
# Deliberately unset. There is no Supabase project yet, and `/v1` answers 503 "not
# configured" until every one of these has a value — a boundary that starts with a guessed
# issuer or a guessed cookie name verifies nothing, so none of them has a default.
#
# Named AUTH_* rather than SUPABASE_* on purpose: doc 14 commits to keeping auth
# JWT-compatible so a move to GoTrue or a WorkOS-style provider needs no token-format
# change, and a provider-shaped variable name is the first thing that quietly breaks that.
# Supabase populates these; it is not named by them.
#
# The accepted signing algorithms are pinned in code, not here. An operator able to widen
# them through configuration could reintroduce algorithm confusion without a code review.

# The provider must issue ASYMMETRIC JWTs (ECC/RSA) and publish a JWKS. The verifier
# refuses symmetric algorithms by construction — a JWKS-backed verifier that accepted
# HS256 is open to algorithm confusion — so an HS256-only project cannot be used here.
#
# Deliberately absent, and not to be added: the service_role key. Every endpoint the
# session transport touches is reachable with the publishable key, and request-path code
# may not hold the privileged one (doc 06 §5).

AUTH_ISSUER=""        # e.g. https://<project>.supabase.co/auth/v1
AUTH_AUDIENCE=""      # the `aud` claim this deployment accepts
AUTH_JWKS_URL=""      # JWKS endpoint; keys are fetched and cached, never hardcoded
AUTH_API_URL=""       # GoTrue REST base, e.g. https://<project>.supabase.co/auth/v1
AUTH_ANON_KEY=""      # publishable key; sent as `apikey` on every provider call
AUTH_COOKIE_NAME=""   # access-token cookie; the refresh cookie is "<name>_refresh"
APP_ORIGIN=""         # this deployment's own origin, for the CSRF Origin check

# ───────────────────────── error reporting (ADR-014 / ADR-015) ─────────────────────────
#
# Deliberately unset here, and an unset value is a NORMAL configuration rather than an
# error: with no DSN the Sentry sink is never registered and logging behaves exactly as it
# did before P1-19 — structured JSON on stdout/stderr, consumed by nothing. A local run has
# no business shipping errors to a vendor, and no test may depend on network egress to one.
#
# A DSN is a write-only ingest key rather than a credential, but it still comes from Doppler
# like everything else (doc 09 §5). A second handling convention for one variable is how the
# first one drifts, and gitleaks runs in CI regardless.
#
# Only `level: "error"` records are forwarded, and only the already-redacted LogRecord is
# sent — never a request, a header, a token, or an address. That is a property of
# `logger.ts`'s ordering, not of anything configured here.
#
# The environment tag is derived from Vercel's own `VERCEL_ENV`, not from a variable of
# ours, so preview deployments do not inflate production's new-issue count.

SENTRY_DSN=""         # unset disables remote error reporting; set per deployed environment

# ───────────────────────────── deployed environments (P1-01) ─────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing below is a new variable. This section says *where each of the above comes from*
# once the application is deployed, because a template that only describes a laptop is how
# a production deployment acquires a laptop's configuration.
#
# Source of truth is Doppler (doc 09 §5), which syncs into Vercel environment variables.
# Set nothing by hand in the Vercel dashboard: a value that exists only there is invisible
# to review, survives no rotation, and is the first thing to drift.
#
# WHICH VARIABLES EACH ENVIRONMENT NEEDS
#
#   every deployed environment   DATABASE_URL, the six AUTH_* values above, SENTRY_DSN
#                                — plus APP_ORIGIN everywhere EXCEPT preview, which
#                                derives it (see below)
#   CI and migrations only       DATABASE_ADMIN_URL
#   local only                   REDIS_URL, DATABASE_EXPECTED_MAJOR
#
# `DATABASE_ADMIN_URL` is deliberately NOT set on the running application in any deployed
# environment. It bypasses RLS by design; the deployment that can reach it is the migration
# job, and the runtime that serves requests is not that job (doc 06 §5).
#
# APP_ORIGIN IS PER-DEPLOYMENT, AND ON VERCEL THAT INCLUDES EVERY PREVIEW.
# It is the origin the CSRF check compares against, so a preview deployment whose
# APP_ORIGIN names production will reject its own form posts.
#
# ON PREVIEW, LEAVE IT UNSET. A stored Vercel variable is a literal string — `$VERCEL_URL`
# in its value is not interpolated — so one stored value cannot follow the different host
# every preview gets. `authConfigFromEnv` therefore derives the origin itself, as
# `https://$VERCEL_URL`, and ONLY when `VERCEL_ENV` is exactly `preview`. Setting
# APP_ORIGIN on preview overrides that and reintroduces the very mismatch it avoids.
#
# EVERY OTHER DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENT MUST SET IT. An explicit value always wins, and outside
# preview there is no fallback at all: a production deployment that loses APP_ORIGIN
# answers 503 rather than accepting its own `*.vercel.app` deployment URL as its origin,
# which would reject every form post from the domain users are actually on.
#
# DATABASE_URL ON SERVERLESS MUST BE THE POOLED CONNECTION.
# Vercel functions scale to many short-lived instances, so the application connects
# through Supabase's transaction-mode pooler, not directly to Postgres. This is already
# what the code assumes (`apps/web/src/server/db.ts` keeps exactly one handle per runtime
# instance "competing for the same transaction-mode pooler"), and it is compatible with
# the RLS design on purpose: the tenant GUC is set with `set_config(..., is_local => true)`
# *inside* a transaction (`packages/db/src/scoped.ts`), so the scope belongs to the
# transaction and cannot leak across a pooled connection.
#
#   application (pooled, port 6543):  postgresql://app_user:<pw>@<host>:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1
#   migrations  (direct, port 5432):  postgresql://<owner>:<pw>@<host>:5432/postgres
#
# Migrations use the direct connection because `prisma migrate deploy` takes advisory
# locks and issues DDL, neither of which survives transaction-mode pooling.
#
# DEPLOYMENT CREDENTIALS ARE NOT APPLICATION CONFIGURATION.
# `VERCEL_TOKEN`, `VERCEL_ORG_ID`, and `VERCEL_PROJECT_ID` are consumed by CI (GitHub
# Actions secrets) and never by the running application, so they are not listed as runtime
# variables here. They are recorded in `docs/architecture/09-infrastructure-and-deployment.md` §9.
